;;; tur/forms/continuations -- capturing "the rest of the computation": shift, call/cc and friends.
;;;
;;; A continuation is the work still to do after some point in a program. These
;;; forms capture it and decide what to do with it: throw it away (an early
;;; exit), call it once, call it many times (backtracking, search), or turn it
;;; into bytes and run it in another process. They differ in how far the capture
;;; reaches and in what the continuation `k` is allowed to do:
;;;
;;;   shift / reset                   up to the nearest `reset`; `k` optional
;;;   shift0                          the abortive `shift` only; takes no `k`
;;;   cloneable-shift / -reset        up to the nearest cloneable-reset; `k` many times
;;;   serial-shift / -reset           as above, and `k` can be written to bytes
;;;   call/cc, escape                 up to the call itself; `k` leaves, once
;;;   call/cc*                        up to the nearest cloneable-reset; `k` many times
;;;
;;; The type of `k` says which kind it is: `cont` (callable any number of times),
;;; `serial-cont`, `escape-cont<T>` (what `call/cc` and `escape` pass; an
;;; unannotated `(fn [k] ...)` gets it) and, for a handler clause, `effect-cont`
;;; (see tur/forms/effects). A continuation is invoked by calling it, `(k v)`;
;;; `resume` is for handler continuations only. The capture forms take a
;;; receiving function, `(shift (fn [k : cont] ...) x)`, not Danvy and Filinski's
;;; `(shift k body)`, which binds a name.
;;; delimited-control-operators-guide compares them; serializable-continuations-guide
;;; covers the serial pair. Several of these forms differ between the compiler
;;; and the interpreter, or are unfinished -- see
;;; docs/reported/compiled-shift-delimited-by-root.md and
;;; docs/upcoming/shift0-pop-plan.md.
;; Reference only: tools/gendocs.py reads the ;;@ lines, the compiler skips them.

;;; reset -- mark how far a shift reaches.
;;;
;;; Evaluates its body forms in order and yields the last one's value, unless a
;;; `shift` inside them (in the body itself or in a function it calls) captures
;;; the context: then the `reset` yields whatever that `shift` produces, and the
;;; rest of the body is gone or is held in `k`. Resets nest; a `shift` belongs to
;;; the nearest. A `reset` takes on the flavor the shifts inside it need: around a
;;; `shift` whose receiver takes a `serial-cont` it is a `serial-reset`, and
;;; around one that takes a `cont` it is a `cloneable-reset`, with their rules on
;;; what may be captured. `call/cc` and `escape` are not stopped by a `reset`:
;;; `(+ 1 (escape (fn [k] (reset (+ 10 (k 5))))))` is 6.

;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body -- one or more expressions
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value of the last body form, or the result of the `shift` that fired.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (reset (+ 1 2))  ; => 3
;;@form (reset body ...)

;;; shift -- capture the context up to the nearest reset.
;;;
;;; `(shift f x)` takes a function `f` and a value `x`. Two shapes, chosen by
;;; the type of `f`'s parameter. With a plain value, `(shift (fn [v : int] (* 2
;;; v)) 10)`, the shift is abortive: the context between the `shift` and the
;;; `reset` is thrown away and the `reset` yields `(f x)`, so
;;; `(reset (+ 1 (shift (fn [v : int] (* 2 v)) 10)))` is 20 -- the `(+ 1 _)` never
;;; happens. `x` must have the type `f` expects (TUR-E0001) and the expression has
;;; `f`'s result type. With a continuation, `(shift (fn [k : cont] ...) x)`, `f`
;;; receives the discarded context as a function `k` and may call it any number
;;; of times: `(reset (+ 1 (shift (fn [k : cont] (k (k 10))) 0)))` is 12. `x` then
;;; only gives the type of the hole; its value is not used. If `f` never uses `k`
;;; the shift is abortive again, and what `f` returns is the value of the `reset`.
;;; The first argument must be a function (`shift requires a function as first
;;; argument`).
;;;
;;; Without any `reset` around it the interpreter stops with `shift used outside
;;; of any reset boundary`; the compiled program reports nothing and behaves
;;; differently according to what else the program contains (it may end the
;;; whole program). Keep every `shift` under a `reset`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking a value, or a `cont`
;;;   x -- the value passed to `f`, or the type of the hole when `f` takes a `cont`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The result type of `f`; at run time the `reset` yields it.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (reset (+ 1 (shift (fn [v : int] (* 2 v)) 10)))  ; => 20
;;;   (reset (+ 1 (shift (fn [k : cont] (k (k 10))) 0)))  ; => 12
;;@form (shift f x)

;;; shift0 -- like shift, but the receiver is not delimited by the reset it took.
;;;
;;; `(shift0 f x)` has the syntax and typing of the abortive `shift`: `f` takes a
;;; value, the context up to the nearest `reset` is discarded, and the `reset`
;;; yields `(f x)`; the expression has `f`'s result type. A receiver that takes a
;;; continuation is TUR-E0001 -- use `shift`. The guides say a `shift0` pops the
;;; reset it captured to, so that a `shift` inside `f` reaches the next one out.
;;; Today that does not show: `(reset (+ 100 (reset (+ 10 (shift0 (fn [v : int]
;;; (shift (fn [w : int] (* 1000 w)) v)) 5)))))` is 5100 on both engines, the
;;; same as with `shift`, where popping the reset would give 5000.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking a value
;;;   x -- the value passed to `f`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The result type of `f`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (reset (shift0 (fn [v : int] (= v 0)) 0))  ; => true
;;@form (shift0 f x)

;;; cloneable-reset -- the boundary for cloneable-shift.
;;;
;;; Evaluates its body forms and yields the last one's value, or what a
;;; `cloneable-shift` inside returned. Everything the continuation captures must
;;; implement `Clone` (TUR-E0014: `captured binding 'x' does not implement
;;; Clone`); a plain integer, float or string always does. A plain `reset` around
;;; a `cloneable-shift` is accepted and has the same effect.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body -- one or more expressions
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value of the last body form, or the result of the `cloneable-shift`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (cloneable-reset (* 2 (cloneable-shift (fn [k : cont] (+ (k 3) (k 4))) 0)))  ; => 14
;;@form (cloneable-reset body ...)

;;; cloneable-shift -- capture the context as a continuation callable many times.
;;;
;;; `(cloneable-shift f x)` calls `f` with the context between here and the nearest
;;; `cloneable-reset` as a `cont`, and the `cloneable-reset` yields what `f`
;;; returns. Calling `(k v)` runs the rest of the body with `v` where the shift
;;; was and returns its value, so `(+ (k 3) (k 4))` in `(* 2 [])` is 6 + 8 = 14,
;;; and `(k (k 10))` in `(+ 1 [])` is 12. `k` can be called any number of times, in
;;; any order; a `f` that never calls it just returns, and its value is the
;;; result of the reset (`99` for `(cloneable-reset (+ 1 (cloneable-shift (fn [k :
;;; cont] 99) 5)))`). `x` gives the type of the hole; its value is not used. The
;;; shift must be inside a `cloneable-reset` (TUR-E0016), and the code between
;;; them must be a shape the native backend can copy: arithmetic, `let` and `if`
;;; around the shift work, but a shift as the middle argument of a call to a
;;; function, `(add3 1 (cloneable-shift ...) 2)`, is TUR-E0710 `cloneable-shift
;;; context is not capturable`, as is a context nested more than a few frames
;;; deep. Compiled and interpreted programs agree on every case here.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking the continuation, `(fn [k : cont] ...)`
;;;   x -- a value whose type is the type of the hole
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The type of the hole; at run time the reset yields what `f` returns.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (cloneable-reset (+ 1 (cloneable-shift (fn [k : cont] (k (k 10))) 0)))  ; => 12
;;@form (cloneable-shift f x)

;;; serial-reset -- the boundary for serial-shift.
;;;
;;; Like `cloneable-reset`, but the continuation can be turned into bytes, so
;;; every value it captures must be `Serializable` (the error is `does not
;;; implement Serializable`). Evaluates its body forms and yields the last one's
;;; value, or what a `serial-shift` returned.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body -- one or more expressions
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value of the last body form, or the result of the `serial-shift`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (serial-reset (+ 10 (serial-shift (fn [k : serial-cont] (k 5)) 0)))  ; => 15
;;@form (serial-reset body ...)

;;; serial-shift -- capture the context as a continuation that can be saved.
;;;
;;; `(serial-shift f x)` calls `f` with the context up to the nearest `serial-reset`
;;; as a `serial-cont` and the reset yields what `f` returns, as `cloneable-shift`
;;; does: `(k v)` runs the rest of the body, as many times as you like. What
;;; `cloneable-shift` cannot do is `stdlib/serial.tur`: `serial-cont->bytes` writes
;;; `k` out, `bytes->serial-cont` reads it back as a `(Result serial-cont cstr)`
;;; (in another process, given the same program), `serial-resume-owned` runs and
;;; frees it, and `cont-to-file` / `cont-from-file` keep the bytes on disk. A
;;; `serial-shift` outside a `serial-reset` is TUR-E0019. Calling `k` works on
;;; both engines; the byte functions are inline C that the interpreter cannot call
;;; with a continuation, so a save and restore runs only in a compiled program.
;;; `x` gives the type of the hole and is not otherwise used.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking the continuation, `(fn [k : serial-cont] ...)`
;;;   x -- a value whose type is the type of the hole
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The type of the hole; at run time the reset yields what `f` returns.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (serial-reset (let [x 42] (+ x (serial-shift (fn [k : serial-cont] (k 100)) 0))))  ; => 142
;;@form (serial-shift f x)

;;; call/cc -- leave a computation early by calling k.
;;;
;;; `(call/cc f)` calls `f` with `k`, a one-shot continuation that leads back to
;;; the `call/cc` itself. If `f` returns, that value is the value of the
;;; `call/cc` (`(+ 1 (call/cc (fn [k] 99)))` is 100). If `f`, or anything it
;;; calls, evaluates `(k v)`, control jumps straight back and the `call/cc` has
;;; the value `v`; the rest of `f` is abandoned: `(+ 1 (call/cc (fn [k] (+ 100
;;; (k 41)))))` is 42. It needs no `reset`, and an intervening `reset` does not
;;; stop it. `k` is valid only while the `call/cc` is running: calling it
;;; afterwards stops the program with `continuation invoked after its call/cc
;;; prompt returned`. The parameter can be written `(fn [k] ...)`, which gives it
;;; the `escape-cont` type, or `(fn [k : escape-cont<int>] ...)`; `^linear` makes
;;; it an error not to call `k` (TUR-E0100) or to call it twice (TUR-E0101),
;;; which an unannotated `k` does not check (it is one-shot by nature: the first
;;; `(k v)` leaves the call). For a continuation that can be called again use
;;; `call/cc*`. The argument must be a function: `(call/cc 5)` is TUR-E0001.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking `k`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value `f` returns, or the value passed to `k`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (+ 1 (call/cc (fn [k] (+ 100 (k 41)))))  ; => 42
;;@form (call/cc f)

;;; call/cc* -- capture a continuation that can be called more than once.
;;;
;;; `(call/cc* f)` calls `f` with the context up to the nearest enclosing
;;; `cloneable-reset` as a `cont`, so it is `(cloneable-shift f 0)` and is what
;;; backtracking and search use. Inside `(cloneable-reset (+ 10 (call/cc* (fn [k :
;;; cont] (+ (k 1) (k 2))))))` the continuation is `(+ 10 _)` and the result is 11
;;; + 12 = 23. As with `cloneable-shift`, an `f` that returns without calling `k`
;;; ends the reset with its value: `(fn [k : cont] 5)` makes the whole reset 5, not
;;; 15 -- unlike `call/cc`, whose normal return continues the surrounding code.
;;; With no enclosing `cloneable-reset` it supplies its own around the call, so
;;; `k` is the identity continuation. The captures must be `Clone` and the
;;; context capturable (TUR-E0014, TUR-E0710), as for `cloneable-shift`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking the continuation, `(fn [k : cont] ...)`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value `f` returns, as the result of the enclosing reset.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (cloneable-reset (+ 10 (call/cc* (fn [k : cont] (+ (k 1) (k 2))))))  ; => 23
;;@form (call/cc* f)

;;; escape -- leave a computation early, with a value.
;;;
;;; `(escape f)` is `call/cc` for the common case of bailing out of deep code:
;;; `f` receives `k`, and `(k v)` unwinds to the `escape` with `v` as its value,
;;; abandoning everything in between. It is the same undelimited, one-shot
;;; capture; the difference the guides draw (`escape` does not put a prompt back
;;; when it unwinds) shows up only for code that mixes it with `shift`, and no
;;; example tried distinguishes the two. Use it for early return without threading
;;; an `Option` through every call. `k` can be passed down to a recursive helper
;;; as an `escape-cont<T>`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   f -- a function taking `k`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value `f` returns, or the value passed to `k`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (+ 1 (escape (fn [k] (+ 100 (k 41)))))  ; => 42
;;;   (defn walk [exit : escape-cont<int> n : int] : int
;;;     (if (= n 0) (exit 7) (+ 1 (walk exit {n - 1}))))
;;;   (println (escape (fn [exit : escape-cont<int>] (walk exit 5))))  ; prints 7
;;@form (escape f)
